    {
      "term": "horse-and-silk trade",
      "matchText": "horse-and-silk trade",
      "type": "concept",
      "definition": "The structured exchange through which the Uyghurs drove herds of steppe horses to the Tang frontier and received back large quantities of silk at a fixed rate they set. Because silk functioned as currency across Inner Asia, this converted the steppe's surplus commodity into universal wealth and drained the Tang treasury for decades."
    },
    {
      "term": "Princess Ningguo",
      "matchText": "Princess Ningguo",
      "definition": "A true-born daughter of the Tang emperor Suzong, sent in 758 to marry the Uyghur khagan — a pointed reversal of the usual marriage-alliance pattern, in which China fobbed steppe rulers off with lesser princesses or palace women rather than a real emperor's daughter. Sending the genuine article measured exactly how badly the post–An Lushan Tang needed Uyghur cavalry.",
      "type": "person"
    },
