    {
      "term": "dual-kaghanate",
      "matchText": "deliberately divided system of senior",
      "definition": "The Kara-Khanid constitutional arrangement in which supreme authority was divided between a senior (Great) Khan ruling the eastern realm from Kashgar and a junior (Subordinate) Khan ruling the west from Balasagun or Samarkand. The system distributed legitimacy across the ruling family to prevent coups, but it also made collective decision-making nearly impossible and left the khanate vulnerable to being picked off vassal-by-vassal by a more unified power — exactly what the Qara-Khitai and then the Mongols did.",
      "type": "concept"
    },
