    {
      "term": "Gurkhan",
      "matchText": "Gurkhan",
      "definition": "A steppe title meaning roughly 'universal khan' or 'khan of khans' — a claim to supreme suzerainty over other rulers rather than merely a single people. Yelü Dashi adopted it in 1132 to announce that the Qara-Khitai stood above the Kara-Khanid khans, the Khwarazm-Shahs, and the other vassal rulers who paid them tribute. It is a deliberate echo of the steppe's most prestigious honorifics, self-consciously positioning a Chinese-style refugee dynasty as the rightful ruler of the Inner Asian world.",
      "type": "concept"
    },
    {
      "term": "Ahmad Sanjar",
      "matchText": "Ahmad Sanjar",
      "wikiSlug": "Ahmad_Sanjar",
      "type": "person"
    },
    {
      "term": "Battle of Qatwan",
      "matchText": "Battle of Qatwan",
      "wikiSlug": "Battle_of_Qatwan",
      "type": "concept"
    },
    {
      "term": "indirect rule",
      "matchText": "tolerant indirect rule",
      "definition": "The Qara-Khitai governing principle of leaving local dynasties — Kara-Khanid khans, Khwarazmian amirs, the Qocho idiqut — in place as tribute-paying vassals supervised by resident commissioners, rather than annexing their territory and installing Qara-Khitai governors. It minimized the cost of garrisoning a vast empire and preserved the tax-paying population's loyalty, but left the center dependent on the cooperation of proud subordinates who could defect the moment the Gurkhans looked weak.",
      "type": "concept"
    },
    {
      "term": "Khwarazm-Shahs",
      "matchText": "Khwarazm-Shahs",
      "wikiSlug": "Khwarazmian_Empire",
      "type": "people"
    },
    {
      "term": "idiqut",
      "matchText": "idiqut",
      "definition": "The title taken by the rulers of the Uyghur Kingdom of Qocho from roughly the tenth century onward — literally something like 'holy fortune' or 'sacred majesty' in Old Uyghur. It replaced the steppe title qaghan and signaled the transformation of a nomadic imperial lineage into a settled agrarian-mercantile kingship. The idiqut of Qocho held the position when the Qara-Khitai absorbed the kingdom, and it was an idiqut — Barchuq — who made the pivotal decision to submit voluntarily to Genghis Khan in 1209.",
      "type": "concept"
    },
    {
      "term": "Juvayni",
      "matchText": "Juvayni",
      "definition": "Ata-Malik Juvayni (1226–1283), a Persian-speaking administrator who served the Mongol Empire and wrote the Ta'rikh-i-Jahan-Gusha ('History of the World Conqueror'), one of the most important primary sources for the Mongol conquests, the Qara-Khitai, and the Khwarazmian Empire. He had access to Mongol court records and the memories of survivors, making him the fullest — if sometimes toadying — account of the late Qara-Khitai period that this chapter relies on.",
      "type": "person"
    },
