    {
      "term": "Chinese-style",
      "matchText": "Chinese-style centralized state",
      "definition": "The Chinese imperial model of government — a salaried, examination-credentialed bureaucracy administering a centralized state through written records, reign-titles, and a codified law, rather than a nomadic confederation parceled among kinsmen. Yelü Dashi carried this template west out of the fallen Liao empire and ran the Qara-Khitai on it, which is why a steppe dynasty taxed and governed Central Asia with a Chinese-style chancery instead of dividing it into tribal appanages.",
      "type": "glossary"
